# `arvo-bits`: a value at an exact declared width, and the bit tier over it

Canon this design declares:
`obligation::an_exact_width_container_a_consumer_can_alias_and_pin`, the need this crate exists to
meet. `ruling::every_family_of_the_old_arvo_returns_and_the_shape_is_open`, under which the bit
contracts return in function, here, with their shape written fresh.
`obligation::set_operations_over_a_fixed_size_bit_set`, whose intersection, union and emptiness test the
bit tier carries up to sixty-four bits. `ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path`, for an
index or an amount past the width being refused at build rather than checked. And
`ruling::ingest_is_the_consumers_and_the_c_abi_is_where_it_ends_up`, the `stated` rung this design sits
under and stays compliant with regardless of how its own open question,
`question::what_the_ingest_row_claims_and_what_turns_on_it`, eventually resolves.

## What this crate is

One type, `Bits<const N: u32>`, holding a value known to be exactly `N` bits wide, and `Bytes<'a>`, the
host's byte slice that is walked into it. It is not a numeral: there is no sign, no ambient domain, no
quantum, no rounding and no arithmetic operation anywhere in this crate. It is the mechanical fact of a
value at a declared width, constructed from a wider host value by masking and cast to a different
declared width by masking again, and the bit operations over that value: reading and writing one bit,
the logic of two values at one width, the emptiness test, and shifting and rotating within the width.

The crate exists because of a gap between what the obligation asks for and what the two crates below
it say about themselves. `arvo-format`'s own design states plainly that "a format here is not a
container" and that there is no machine carrier reachable from it. `arvo-placement`'s own design
states that it derives placement metadata, three widths and an occupancy, and is checked against ever
holding a host primitive itself, because it does not introduce the numeric category. Neither crate,
by its own stated charter, can hold a runtime value at a declared width. This crate is that hole,
closed narrowly: a value, not a placement and not a format, with the operations a bit container has.

## Why it builds on `arvo-format`

The bit tier answers in a truth value and in a count of bits, and `arvo-format` is where both are
defined: `arvo_format::Bool` and `arvo_format::Width`. So this crate depends on it, and every predicate
here returns a `Bool` and the width comes back as a `Width`, rather than this crate defining a second
truth value or a second count beside them.

The width is a capability now where it was not before. Nothing in the crate erases `N`, but a consumer
generic over the bit tier does: inside a function bounded by `BitAccess`, the container's `N` is not in
scope, and the bound is the only thing that can give it back. A scheduler's read set and a compiler's
artifact set are written exactly that way, over whichever container the caller picked.

The edge costs a consumer of `Bits<28>` the compile of `arvo-format`, and that is paid once per build.
It does not make `arvo-format` a container or put a carrier in reach of it: the dependency runs from
the container to the format's vocabulary, and the format still names no carrier.

## Why the width bound is not `arvo_format::slots::Slots`

`Slots` states a bound that reads the same as this one, admitted at widths one to sixty-four. The two
bounds agree in number and are still not the same claim. `Slots`'s bound is the widest pointer width a
target has and the widest carrier the placement ladder offers; its slot index sits in a signed 128-bit
integer that would go on well past it. `Bits<N>`'s bound exists because the value is carried in a
`u64`, which runs out of capacity at `64`; that is a storage-capacity overflow. Either could move
without the other having a reason to, so this crate asserts its own bound independently rather than
borrowing it from a trait answering a different question.

## The type

```
Bits<const N: u32>(u64)
```

`repr(transparent)`. `N` is bounded to the closed range one to sixty-four by a private `ADMITTED: ()`
constant, asserted inside a `const` item and forced by every public constructor and cast, the same
codegen-forced-assert shape `arvo_format::slots::Slots::ADMITTED` already uses: the assertion is
evaluated where the const is used, so a call reaches it at codegen and `cargo build` refuses a bad `N`
where `cargo check` would not have. `ADMITTED` needs no public visibility here, unlike on `Slots`;
`Bits<N>` is a concrete type, and only its own constructors force the check.

Construction masks. `Bits::<N>::masked(raw: u64) -> Self` is a bitwise AND against a precomputed mask
and nothing else, total by construction: there is no input it rejects, because there is no invalid
input, only one whose bits above `N` get dropped. This is deliberate and is the reason this crate never
needed a `TryFrom` or a `notko::Boundable` impl. Both of those are shaped around rejecting a value, and
the ruling this design sits under records op declining, in terms, to have arvo own a validating
constructor for exactly this kind of boundary. Shipping one here under a different name, even for an
in-memory value rather than untrusted external bytes, would be the same door with a different sign on
it.

`Bits::<N>::raw(self) -> u64` is the unwrap door, the one place a host contract needs the value back,
matching the pattern `arvo_format::Width::count`, `arvo_format::slots::Slot::index` and
`arvo_format::Bool::get` already use.

`Bits::<N>::cast<const M: u32>(self) -> Bits<M>` is `Bits::<M>::masked(self.raw())`: the same masking
arithmetic at a different bound, which covers both directions with one operation rather than a widen
method and a separate narrow method for what is structurally one fact. Widening (`M > N`) never drops a
bit, because the wider mask is a strict superset of the narrower one. Narrowing (`M < N`) drops the
bits above `M`, which is exactly what a masked cast at a boundary the consumer controls already means
in the consumer's own vocabulary, and is not a defect this design tries to prevent.

`Bits::<64>::halves(raw: u128) -> [Bits<64>; 2]` splits a 128-bit host value into its low half and its
high half, in that order. A value wider than the carrier is held as an array of carriers rather than
as a wider `Bits`, because the carrier stops at sixty-four, and this door is where the host's 128-bit
integer meets that array.

## The bit tier

Four traits, declared here and implemented here for `Bits<N>`. They are the container's own contract,
which is why they sit with the container rather than with the format whose `Bool` and `Width` they
answer in: `arvo-format` holds formats and states it is not a container, and a container's contract
declared there would contradict it. Another container, an array of `Bits<64>` for a wider set among
them, implements the same traits in its own crate.

Every operation is an inherent `const fn` on `Bits<N>`, and each trait is a `const` trait whose impl
for `Bits<N>` calls the inherent method and does nothing else. A consumer naming `Bits<N>` calls the
method and needs no feature of the toolchain; a consumer generic over the tier enables
`const_trait_impl` where it evaluates the tier in a constant, and an ordinary generic function needs
nothing. `mock/research/sketches/202611200200_bit-tier-const-trait/` holds the shape building on the
pinned nightly.

`BitWidth` carries `const WIDTH: Width`, the declared width, `Width::bits(N)` for `Bits<N>`.

`BitAccess` reads and writes one bit, with `BitWidth` as its supertrait. `bit::<const I: u32>(self) ->
Bool` is whether bit `I` is set, counting from the low bit at zero. `with_bit::<const I: u32>(self, to:
Bool) -> Self` is the same value with bit `I` set to `to` and every other bit as it was.

`BitLogic` is the logic of two values at one width. `and`, `or` and `xor` take `(self, other: Self) ->
Self` and `not` takes `(self) -> Self`, each the host's bitwise operation held within `N` bits: `not`
masks, since the host's complement sets the bits above the width, and the other three need no mask,
since both operands are already within it. `is_empty(self) -> Bool` is whether no bit is set. No bit of
any of these depends on another bit position, so nothing carries and there is no policy to choose.
Both sides are one width, and a consumer combining two widths casts one first, which keeps the choice
of width the consumer's.

`BitSequence` moves the bits within the width, by an amount that is a const generic.
`shift_up::<const K: u32>(self) -> Self` and `shift_down::<const K: u32>(self) -> Self` move every bit
`K` places toward the high bit and toward the low bit, dropping what leaves the width and bringing in
zeros. `rotate_up::<const K: u32>(self) -> Self` and `rotate_down::<const K: u32>(self) -> Self` move
the same way and bring what leaves one end in at the other, within `N` bits rather than within the
carrier, so a rotation of a `Bits<28>` wraps at bit twenty-seven. An amount of zero is the identity.

An index `I` or an amount `K` at or past `N` is refused at build. A private `const` item over `N` and the
index asserts it, forced inside the inherent method, the shape `ADMITTED` has, so the refusal is
`E0080` at `cargo build` through the inherent method, through a `const fn` generic over the trait and
through an ordinary generic function alike. Nothing is checked at run time.

The intersection test `obligation::set_operations_over_a_fixed_size_bit_set` names is
`a.and(b).is_empty()`, union is `or`, and the emptiness test is `is_empty`. On the 64-bit carrier the
first is one `test` and the other two are one instruction each; the round that brought the tier
records the disassembly rather than this paragraph asserting it.

## The byte run: `Bytes<'a>`

`Bytes<'a>` is a borrowed run of the host's bytes, `repr(transparent)` over `&'a [u8]`. It is the door
the host's byte image enters the stack by, and it sits in this crate because this crate already
introduces the container a byte is read into. `Bytes::of(&'a [u8])` wraps the slice and `Bytes::host`
gives it back, for the one place a host contract needs it, such as a library taking a slice.

A run is walked by `split_first`, which returns `notko::Maybe<(Bits<8>, Bytes<'a>)>`: the first byte
and the rest, or `Isnt` when the run is empty. Walking needs no index type and no length, and the walk
is a `const fn`, so a digest over it can be. Nothing here asks a run's length; a consumer that needs
it at a boundary adds the question with its use.

## What the ruling constrains, and how this design stays inside it without resolving it

`ruling::ingest_is_the_consumers_and_the_c_abi_is_where_it_ends_up` carries op's words that arvo may
ship casting and conversion helpers and may not use them in place of the consumer. Its own `note`
records that he was asked to bless the row and declined, and that a panel question,
`question::what_the_ingest_row_claims_and_what_turns_on_it`, is open over what the row concretely
constrains, among four candidate readings. That question is not this design's to answer; its decider
is the panel. What this design does instead is stay correct under every reading at once: nothing in
this crate calls `masked` or `cast` on a consumer's behalf, and neither can fail, so there is no
validating constructor for any reading to refuse and no entry point that decides an ingest question the
consumer did not decide themselves.

## What this crate does not ship

No hashing algorithm (`arvo-digest` holds those, built on this crate), no `arvo-hash` crate, no `ContentHash` alias, no `rkyv::Archive` implementation.
The obligation asks for a container a consumer can "alias under its own name," which is the consumer
doing the aliasing; nothing here pre-empts that by shipping a domain alias of its own. A downstream
crate wanting `pub type ContentHash = arvo_bits::Bits<28>;` writes exactly that line, in its own crate,
whenever it wants it.

Nothing past sixty-four bits. A set of 256 bits is an array of `Bits<64>` and implements the tier in the
crate that holds it, and whether its intersection test still lowers to one instruction is the open half
of the set obligation's own `gap`.

No shift by an amount known only at run time. A total one, where an amount past the width gives zero,
lowers either to a comparison or to the host's shift, which wraps the amount, and which of the two arvo
ships is not settled by anything this design declares.

No host door for a numeral: `arvo-numeral` has one at every width and both signs.

## The surface this design names

`Bits<const N: u32>`, `Bytes<'a>`, and the traits `BitWidth`, `BitAccess`, `BitLogic` and `BitSequence`
are the whole public surface. On `Bits`, `masked` constructs, `raw` reads the value back at its natural
host width, `cast` reinterprets at a different declared width, and `halves` splits a 128-bit host value;
`bit`, `with_bit`, `and`, `or`, `xor`, `not`, `is_empty`, `shift_up`, `shift_down`, `rotate_up` and
`rotate_down` are inherent `const fn`s, and the four traits are implemented for every `Bits<N>`. `Clone`,
`Copy`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `PartialOrd`, `Ord`, `Hash`, `Debug` are derived, matching the derive set
`arvo_format::Width` and `arvo_format::slots::Slot` already carry, with `Hash` added because a consumer
using this as a lookup key (a content hash into a string table, named directly in the obligation's own
`why`) needs it. `Bytes` has `of`, `host` and `split_first`, and derives `Clone`, `Copy`, `PartialEq`,
`Eq` and `Debug`.

## The laws this crate owes tests for

Construction never rejects. There is no input to `masked` that fails; the compile-time refusal is
on `N` itself, at construction of the type, not on any runtime value handed to it.

`masked` and `raw` round-trip exactly for any value already within `N` bits. For `raw < 2^N`,
`Bits::<N>::masked(raw).raw() == raw`.

`masked` drops exactly the bits above `N` for any value: `Bits::<N>::masked(raw).raw() == raw &
((1u64 << N) - 1)` for `N < 64`, and `== raw` for `N == 64`, where the mask computation takes the
shift-overflow case explicitly rather than relying on `1u64 << 64` not panicking.

`cast` composes with `masked` rather than adding a second rule: `b.cast::<M>() ==
Bits::<M>::masked(b.raw())` for every `b: Bits<N>` and every admitted `M`, including `M == N`.

`N` outside `1..=64` is refused at `cargo build`, not at `cargo check`. Pinned as a `trybuild`
`compile_fail` case at `N == 0` and at `N == 65`, mirroring `arvo_format::slots`'s own
`width_above_the_bound.rs`.

`halves` loses nothing: the low half is the low sixty-four bits of `raw`, the high half the rest, and
the two shifted and ored back give `raw`, over values with only the low half set, only the high half
set, both, and neither.

`split_first` walks a run in order and ends: walking `Bytes::of(s)` to `Isnt` yields exactly the bytes
of `s`, each as a `Bits<8>`, for the empty run and for a run of every byte value.

The tier's laws are stated against the host's own operation on the carrier, and are tested for every
value, index and amount at every width up to eight, and at the widths above over values at the edges of
the width and seeded ones.

`WIDTH` is `N`.

`bit::<I>` is bit `I` of `raw`. `with_bit::<I>(to)` reads back `to` at `I` and leaves every other bit as
it was, and writing a bit to what it already is gives the value back.

`and`, `or` and `xor` equal the host's on `raw`. `not` is the host's complement masked to `N` bits, and
is an involution. The three binary operations commute and associate, `xor` with itself is the empty
value, and De Morgan holds: `a.and(b).not() == a.not().or(b.not())`. `is_empty` is true exactly at
zero, and `a.and(b).is_empty()` is true exactly when no bit is set in both.

`shift_up::<K>` is the host's left shift by `K` masked to `N` bits, and `shift_down::<K>` the host's
right shift by `K`. `rotate_up::<K>` followed by `rotate_down::<K>` is the identity, `rotate_up::<K>`
equals `rotate_down::<{ N - K }>` for `K` other than zero, and a rotation sets as many bits as it was
given.

Each trait method agrees with its inherent method, through a function generic over the trait, and the
tier evaluates in a constant through a `const fn` generic over `[const]` bounds.

An index or an amount at `N` is refused at `cargo build`, pinned as `trybuild` `compile_fail` cases:
`bit`, `with_bit`, a shift and a rotation through the inherent method, and `bit` through a function
generic over `BitAccess`.
